install: make every stream durable before INST DONE and before a reset

The P1 installer wrote the userland through the UBC and reported INST DONE
without a VOP_FSYNC, without a filesystem sync and without a device cache
flush.  A 1.04 GB stream at 7.84 MiB/s leaves up to ~235 MB inside a 30 s
write-back window, so the next phase could boot from a truncated .symbols or
cache shard and report a dyld/codesign failure instead of a lost write.
FASTBOOT then went straight to PEHaltRestart() with no VFS sync at all.

Flush at both transitions:

  - VNOP_FSYNC(MNT_WAIT) on every regular-file vnode while it is still held,
    so HFS+ pushes the clusters and the catalog record before close;
  - at stream completion, VFS_SYNC(root mount, MNT_WAIT) followed by the same
    SYNCHRONIZE CACHE(10) kebab_vol.c uses, and only then INST DONE;
  - a cold sample check after that sync: the first, largest, middle and last
    files have their UBC pages invalidated and are read again.  Small files
    are hashed in full; a large file is checked by size plus the digest of
    its last 4 KiB.  There is no device-level read for an arbitrary file
    because the extent map is private to HFS+, so this is a cache-dropped
    VFS read, not a raw block read;
  - before FASTBOOT/FASTBOOT2/REBOOT, kebab_install_sync_for_reset() runs
    VFS_SYNC and SYNCHRONIZE CACHE and refuses the reset if either fails.
    The panic path calls ud_platform_restart() directly and is unchanged.

INST CHECK and the per-entry read-back stay UBC-backed; the stream-end sync
is what makes their contents media-backed, and the cold sample is the proof
for a few files.

